8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,4,1,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,1,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,1,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,1,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,1,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"Each grid contains one or more small three-part scenes, and I treat each one on its own.\n\n**What the pieces are**\n\n- **The line.** A straight bar of one colour, for example the 8s in pair 1, the 2s in pairs 2 and 3, the 5s in pair 4 and the 6s in pair 5.\n- **The snake.** An object of another colour, connected through corners. One of its cells sits right beside an end cell of the line, to the side of the line rather than along it. That cell is the snake's anchor.\n- **The target.** A third object nearby: the 2 in pair 1, the 4s in pair 2, the 9s in pair 3, the 3-chevron in pair 4 and the 3s in pair 5.\n\n**What changes**\n\nIn the output the line and the target stay put. The snake is erased and redrawn as a new path of the same colour:\n\n1. The path starts at the anchor and keeps the same cell count as the original snake. It is cut off at the grid edge: pair 1 has 6 snake cells but only 4 fit, and pair 4 has 6 cells but only 5 fit.\n2. Each step moves one row further past the line's end, continuing in the line's direction.\n3. The sideways position follows a fixed pattern:\n   - **Approach.** Before reaching the target's rows, the path shifts by at most one cell per step toward the column just beside the target. That column is on the same side of the line as the snake. In pair 1 the path is already there, so it goes straight up.\n   - **Hug.** In the target's rows, the path sits exactly one cell outside the target's outermost cell on that side. This copies the target's outline; pair 4 shows this with the chevron.\n   - **Leave.** After the target, the path drifts back one cell per row. This makes the diagonal tail seen in pairs 1, 3 and 5.\n\n**How it fits every pair**\n\n- Pair 3: the path goes 7→8→9→10, holds at 10 for three rows, then 9→8→7. That is 9 cells, matching the snake's 9.\n- Pair 2: the path goes 6→5→4→3→3.\n- Pair 5: the path goes 4→3→2→2→3→4.\n\n**How it applies to the tests**\n\nTo handle lines in any orientation, I measure positions along the line's direction and across it, toward the snake's side.\n\n- Test 1 has two scenes: line 3 with the 8-snake and the 9-blob as target, and line 1 with the 4-snake and the single 6 as target.\n- Test 2 has three scenes: line 3 with the 1-snake and the 9s, line 2 with the 5-snake and the 6s (here the line is horizontal), and line 1 with the 4-snake and the 5-block.\n- For each snake, the target is the closest object that is not a line and not a snake.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True)\n    owner = {}\n    for i, o in enumerate(objs):\n        for c in o[\"cells\"]:\n            owner[c] = i\n\n    def straight(o):\n        ys = {c[0] for c in o[\"cells\"]}; xs = {c[1] for c in o[\"cells\"]}\n        return o[\"size\"] >= 2 and (len(ys) == 1 or len(xs) == 1)\n\n    scenes = []\n    used = set()\n    for li, o in enumerate(objs):\n        if not straight(o):\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        if x0 == x1:\n            ends = [((y0, x0), (-1, 0)), ((y1, x0), (1, 0))]\n        else:\n            ends = [((y0, x0), (0, -1)), ((y0, x1), (0, 1))]\n        for (ey, ex), d in ends:\n            perps = [(d[1], d[0]), (-d[1], -d[0])]\n            for n in perps:\n                sy, sx = ey + n[0], ex + n[1]\n                if 0 <= sy < H and 0 <= sx < W and (sy, sx) in owner:\n                    si = owner[(sy, sx)]\n                    if si != li and objs[si][\"colour\"] != o[\"colour\"]:\n                        scenes.append((li, si, (sy, sx), d, n))\n                        used.add(li); used.add(si)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for li, si, s, d, n in scenes:\n        snake = objs[si]\n        cands = [i for i in range(len(objs)) if i not in used]\n        if not cands:\n            continue\n        ti = min(cands, key=lambda i: min(max(abs(c[0]-s[0]), abs(c[1]-s[1])) for c in objs[i][\"cells\"]))\n        A = lambda c: c[0]*d[0] + c[1]*d[1]\n        Q = lambda c: c[0]*n[0] + c[1]*n[1]\n        side = {}\n        for c in objs[ti][\"cells\"]:\n            a = A(c); side[a] = max(side.get(a, -10**9), Q(c) + 1)\n        amin, amax = min(side), max(side)\n        for c in snake[\"cells\"]:\n            out[c] = bg\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Erase the colour-{snake['colour']} snake attached to the line end.\")\n        a, q = A(s), Q(s)\n        path = [(a, q)]\n        for t in range(1, snake[\"size\"]):\n            a += 1\n            if a < amin:\n                goal = side[amin]\n                q += (goal > q) - (goal < q)\n            elif a <= amax:\n                q = side.get(a, q)\n            else:\n                q -= 1\n            path.append((a, q))\n        for a, q in path:\n            y = a*d[0] + q*n[0]; x = a*d[1] + q*n[1]\n            if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n                out[y, x] = snake[\"colour\"]\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Redraw the snake as a path wrapping around the colour-{objs[ti]['colour']} target.\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The snake is erased and redrawn as a new path of the same colour:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The path starts at the anchor and keeps the same cell count as the original snake."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Approach.** Before reaching the target's rows, the path shifts by at most one cell per step toward the column just beside the target."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The path starts at the anchor and keeps the same cell count as the original snake."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Approach.** Before reaching the target's rows, the path shifts by at most one cell per step toward the column just beside the target."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Leave.** After the target, the path drifts back one cell per row."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"b478d82ac08a2b216457677dcbd4e6cfb94bdd550f3a3f72f9e3d4432b99429f","task_sha256":"fb24a105512d4cdc604e8b5295fd80f5a40a626af7f86767b98a351ce5fc2777","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
